{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:scout","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:3b446e3aa8396d41768518aa843323cc862e2906040d4cd3e89d864f3fae2729","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.2},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.1},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.1}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"OBSERVED ANCHOR (locked, treated as given fact): US 10y TIPS real yield ~2.60%, FRED DFII10, 30 Oct 2026. That is the highest sustained US real yield since 2008 and it is the single most important number in this question.\n\nNON-US LEGS ARE WHERE I HAVE TO ADMIT IGNORANCE. G7 GDP weights (nominal USD): US ~0.50, Japan ~0.11, Germany ~0.11, UK ~0.08, France ~0.08, Canada ~0.065, Italy ~0.055. What I could verify: US 10y nominal 4.44% on 18 June 2026 (stale); 10y JGB \"rose to a 30-year high\" on 1 Sep 2026 per Reuters, level not given in the excerpt — unverified at my horizon; a 10y JGB print of 2.06% described as a 26-year high in Dec-2025; a 2.635% bund level that is UNDATED and which I therefore will not rely on. Current November-2026 levels for any G7 benchmark other than the locked US TIPS print are unverified at my horizon.\n\nINFERRED CURRENT LEVEL (clearly separated from observation): using the locked 2.60% US print plus my best estimates of the others (Japan ~0.0-0.3% real, Germany ~0.3-0.8%, UK ~1.5-2.0%, France ~1.0-1.3%, Italy ~1.5%, Canada ~1.5%), the G7 GDP-weighted 10y real yield today is ~1.80%. The US contributes 1.30pp of that 1.80pp, so this question is ~72% a US question.\n\nWALK-FORWARD TO 2032-36. Pushing UP, all from the locked block plus live telemetry: global (r-g) worsens ~+1.0pp even where a frictionless r* eases; gross public debt crosses 100% of GDP by 2029, ~142% US and ~127% China by 2031; net interest 2%->3% of GDP; 3-year P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% of GDP, +2.4pp more under a 20% AI-led equity valuation shock; eroding sovereign convenience yields; retiree dissaving; and an enormous verified real-capex claim on savings (hyperscaler 2026 capex forecast $803-828B with executives pledging more in 2027, Amazon \"meaningfully more in 2027\", Alphabet 2026 raised to $180-190B and 2027 to increase \"significantly\", Moody's at ~$785B this year closing on $1T in 2027 — note the $803-828B and the earlier ~$725B Big-4 figures use different company sets and dates and are not directly comparable). Pushing DOWN: the BIS AER 2026 Box C aggregate-demand-shortfall channel, post-bust excess capacity, slower g, and financial repression (regulatory captive demand for sovereigns).\n\nP50 = 2.1%: a modest further rise from the inferred 1.80%, driven mostly by Japan and Germany normalizing off near-zero real yields rather than by the US going much above 2.60%. P10 = 1.2% (D-regime: demand shortfall plus repression pulls the weighted real yield back toward the 2010s). P90 = 3.1% (C-regime term-premium spike). Five-year averages compress tails, which is why I do not go above 3.1 or below 1.2.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.210Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Atlanta Fed Wage Growth Tracker, 12-month moving average of median hourly wage growth: the Construction-and-Mining series MINUS the Finance-and-Business-Services series. Current observed state is a dead heat on the closest available proxies (Education and Health 4.2 in Sep-2025; Finance and Business Services 4.2 in Feb-2026; Construction and Mining latest value unverified at my horizon). Watch for a gap exceeding +1.5pp sustained across four consecutive quarters. Caveat: these are broad industry groups, not ISIC F/Q/J/K/M, and they are unweighted medians.\",\"by_when\":\"Q4 2